1. GRANT OF LICENSE
Subject to the terms and conditions of this Agreement and the timely payment of
applicable licensing fees, HydroGo Licensor grants to the Customer a non-exclusive,
non-transferable, revocable license to install, execute, and utilize HydroGo Core
in binary or private module form, strictly within the scope of the purchased
Edition (e.g., Starter, Growth, Enterprise, Custom) and Project ID specified in
the cryptographically signed offline license certificate ("hydro.lic").

2. RESTRICTIONS ON USE
Except as explicitly authorized by Licensor in writing, Customer shall NOT:
  a. Decompile, disassemble, reverse engineer, decrypt, or attempt to derive the
     source code of HydroGo Core runtime components, except to the extent
     mandated by applicable non-waivable laws;
  b. Modify, forge, bypass, circumvent, or disable the cryptographic license
     verification system or Ed25519 signature checks;
  c. Sub-license, lease, rent, redistribute, resell, or provide HydroGo Core as
     an unbundled standalone software product or third-party SaaS without a
     valid Partnership or Reseller agreement;
  d. Remove or alter any copyright notices, proprietary markings, or license
     validation mechanisms embedded in HydroGo.

3. CLIENT EDITION AND GENERATED CODE
Source code generated exclusively for the Customer's project (including Client
Editions, theme templates, migrations, frontend layouts, and custom extension
hooks) is owned by Customer or licensed according to the applicable Client
Edition delivery contract, provided that running or building such code with
HydroGo Core requires a valid, active HydroGo license.

4. OFFLINE VERIFICATION AND GRACE PERIOD
HydroGo utilizes an offline cryptographic license verification mechanism. In the
event of license expiration, a defined grace period may permit temporary
operational continuity while renewal is processed. Continued operation after the
grace period has elapsed constitutes an unlicensed use of the software.

5. THIRD-PARTY COMPONENTS
HydroGo incorporates third-party open-source components. The licensing terms of
such third-party components are detailed in the accompanying NOTICE file and
remain governed by their respective open-source licenses.

